  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Leaving Spain shows early but clear signs of use and relevance: it has been downloaded 1,453 times through open-access distribution, is aligned with the Sustainable Development Goal on reduced inequalities, and addresses migration, inequality, and social change in contemporary Europe. The book’s record is still relatively quiet in citation-based measures, so its strongest evidence at present is readership rather than scholarly uptake.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2022, Leaving Spain examines the post-2008 departure of young people from Spain through 58 autobiographical narrative interviews with migrants to the UK and Germany, including some who later returned. That design places the book squarely in biographical and narrative research, while also connecting to sociology, political science, geography, gender studies, and broader social-science questions about mobility and inequality.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is usage. Open-access metrics show sustained downloading, indicating that the book is being actively consulted by readers rather than merely listed in a catalogue. Its open availability through institutional and repository infrastructure likely supports that reach, and the book is also indexed as openly accessible in the available repository record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The thematic relevance is reinforced by its alignment with reduced inequalities. That fit is meaningful for a study of migration driven by economic संकट and unequal opportunity, since the book speaks directly to how labour markets, precarity, and transnational mobility shape life chances. The funding record further suggests that the project was supported across multiple institutional and international sources, including KU Leuven, Stiftung der Deutschen Wirtschaft, and Cambridge Commonwealth European and International Trust.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record is still at an early stage, with no OpenAlex citations recorded in the digest. That does not diminish the book’s substantive contribution; it simply means that the most visible evidence currently comes from readership and thematic relevance rather than from accumulated scholarly citation. The available record therefore portrays Leaving Spain as a socially engaged monograph with demonstrated access and use, and with potential for broader scholarly uptake over time.</p>",
